\description{
\code{spicy()} tests, for every ordered pair of cell types \code{from} → \code{to}, whether the co-localisation of
the two types differs between conditions, or is associated with survival. A pair asks whether \code{to}
cells are placed near \code{from} cells more than other cells are.
}
\details{
\strong{\code{method = "cell"} (the default).} In each image, the share of \code{to} cells with at least one \code{from}
cell within radius \code{r} is compared with its exact expectation q if the \code{to} cells were a random choice
among the cells that are not \code{from} cells. The effect (\code{effect = "allocation"}, the default) is the
\strong{fraction of \code{to} cells placed next to (or kept away from) \code{from} cells}. For a pair that attracts
(more \code{to} cells next to \code{from} cells than q over all images together) it is (observed share - q) / (1 - q):
if a fraction f of the \code{to} cells were moved next to \code{from} cells, the effect is f. For a pair that avoids
it is (observed share - q) / q: if a fraction f of the \code{to} cells that would have a \code{from} cell nearby were
moved away, the effect is -f. Either way it does not depend on how many \code{from} cells there are or how
densely they are packed. The side is chosen once per pair from all images with a condition, without the
conditions, and is reported in the \code{side} column. \code{effect = "count"} gives the number of extra \code{from}
cells within \code{r} of each \code{to} cell instead; it also reflects how many \code{from} cells surround a \code{to} cell
(depth of infiltration), but it grows with how densely the \code{from} cells are packed, so a change in
packing alone can appear as a change in co-localisation. Images are combined within patients and
patients within conditions by a frailty GEE with a second random effect for the images of a patient (each image
is weighted by 1 / (its sampling variance + the between-image variance), so one large image cannot outweigh the
patient's other images), and the difference between conditions is tested with a
CR2 cluster-robust variance on Satterthwaite degrees of freedom, with \strong{patients (\code{subject}) as the
units}. The difference is adjusted for any \code{covariates}, and, with \code{adjustAbundance = TRUE}, for the
log share of the \code{from} type in each image; the unadjusted test is then reported alongside
(\verb{unadjusted_*} columns).

When nearly every cell has a \code{from} cell within \code{r} (q close to 1), there is little room for attraction and
an attracting pair's images carry little information; a smaller \code{r} is more informative.

The allocation effect is bounded: an image of an avoiding pair with no \code{to} cell next to a \code{from} cell has the
effect -1, and an image of an attracting pair with every \code{to} cell next to one has +1. A patient is informative at
a radius when at least one of its images is not at -1 or +1. A radius where one condition has no informative
patient is not tested: that condition's patients all have the same effect, so its spread is exactly 0 and the
standard error would rest on the other condition alone. Its p-value is \code{NA} (with adjustment, \code{adjusted_for} is
\code{"none (no_information)"}), and it is left out of the combination over radii. With more than two conditions, a
pair where any condition has no informative patient is not tested.

The standard error of the difference is the sum of each condition's own CR2 variance, on Satterthwaite degrees of
freedom; with adjustment, the CR2 variance of the adjusted design. \code{variance = "hartung_knapp"} keeps its own
variance.

For a self-pair (\code{from} = \code{to}) every cell can carry the label, so random labelling moves both the cells counted
and their neighbours. Its variance is the exact random-labelling variance of the self-pair (for the radius graph
and for the k nearest neighbours), not the variance of a pair of two types.

With the allocation effect, an image whose room on the pair's side (the \code{to} cells without a \code{from} neighbour
expected by chance for an attracting pair, those with one for an avoiding pair) is below one cell is not used:
its effect could not show the pair's side, and could be large in the other direction. The side is chosen first,
from every image.

A patient's weight within its condition grows with the information of its images, so one or two patients can
carry nearly all of a condition's weight. A radius where a condition's effective patients (Kish's
(sum w)^2 / sum w^2 of the patients' weights w) fall below the smaller of 3 and half the number of its patients
that contribute at that radius is not tested: its p-value is \code{NA} (with adjustment, \code{adjusted_for} is
\code{"none (few_effective_units)"}), and it is left out of the combination over radii. A condition of 3 patients with
equal weights can therefore be tested. With more than two conditions, a pair where any condition falls below this
is not tested.

Images with a missing condition are left out of every step: the choice of the side, the between-image
variance and the tests.

\strong{\code{method = "image"} (the original spicyR test).} A per-image L-function summary of each pair is
compared between conditions with a weighted linear model, or a mixed model when \code{subject} is given
(Canete et al. 2022). With \code{cellWeights} (see \code{\link[=getPairwise]{getPairwise()}}), cells enter each label with a weight,
for example the confidence of a classifier, instead of through a single cell type.
}
\examples{
data("diabetesData")
# spicyR Cell: patients ("case") are the units
# the extra fraction of Th cells, and of beta cells, with a Tc cell within 50 units
res <- spicy(diabetesData, condition = "stage", subject = "case", r = 50,
             from = "Tc", to = c("Th", "beta"))
topPairs(res)
res$cellResults

# the extra number of Tc cells within 50 units of each Th cell
resCount <- spicy(diabetesData, condition = "stage", subject = "case", r = 50,
                  from = "Tc", to = "Th", effect = "count")

# the original image-level test
resImage <- spicy(diabetesData, condition = "stage", subject = "case",
                  from = "Tc", to = "Th", method = "image")
topPairs(resImage)
}
